//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: nested closures work when the inner closure captures its
//@ reason: IMMEDIATELY ENCLOSING closure's own PARAMETER (`x`, from the
//@ reason: outer's `(int x)` parameter list) rather than the outer's OWN
//@ reason: capture (`total`) -- see closure_nested_rejected.clg for the
//@ reason: negative control proving the latter is still, correctly,
//@ reason: rejected. A parameter is an ordinary local already in the outer
//@ reason: closure's method body, not reached through `this` at all, so
//@ reason: capturing it needs no new borrow/ownership machinery: it is
//@ reason: captured by value exactly like a top-level function's own
//@ reason: parameter already is. Implementation: ASTBuilder::desugarClosures
//@ reason: (via closure_desugar.cpp's desugarOne) now walks a closure's OWN
//@ reason: body with a scope seeded from that closure's OWN parameter list
//@ reason: BEFORE moving the body into the synthesized override method --
//@ reason: this is what lets the existing recursive rewriteExpr/walkBody
//@ reason: machinery find and desugar the nested closure at all, with the
//@ reason: inner closure's synthesized class emitted BEFORE the outer's
//@ reason: (required: CeeLing has no forward declarations, and the outer's
//@ reason: own method body references the inner class by name). Must be
//@ reason: RUN: confirms the inner closure's dispatch actually receives the
//@ reason: right value carried through two levels of indirection, not just
//@ reason: that it compiles.
//@ run: 2

class Callback {
public:
    Callback() { }
    virtual void call(int x) { }
};

void each(Callback cb) {
    cb.call(1);
}

int main() {
    int total = 100;
    each(closure Callback [total] (int x) {
        each(closure Callback [x] (int y) {
            std::cout << (x + y) << std::endl;
        });
    });
    return 0;
}
